[Music]  [Music]  okay  welcome to the press conference  following the eastern partnership summit  we have a translation into many  languages so please tune in also in  russian by the way  most importantly we have just issued the  join declaration on our council webpage  and i invite you to read it we're going  to send you the link via signal as well  and i please give the floor first to  president michelle  merci merci beaucoup  madam president thank you  thank you charles  indeed i'm also glad that we had  finally the the opportunity to have a  physical meeting with our eastern  partners  because the current geopolitical  situation really demands it and indeed  we were discussing the current  challenges but also our the future of  our partnership and i can pick up where  you just left indeed there was an empty  chair in the room  and  i hope that this chair will soon be  filled by a legitimate democratically  elected belarusian leader  we all know that the belarusian regime  represses its own people  it instrumentalizes migrants putting  their lives at risk  and it is launching in parallel a hybrid  attack against the european union  you know that we responded to this  attack rapidly forcefully and in all  unity we introduced sanctions  we coordinated with our partners mainly  the united states canada and the united  kingdom  we prepared the black listing of  transport companies involved in the  smuggling of migrants  we increased our financial support to  latvia lithuania and poland on border  management  we're working with the united nations  agencies to support  the people trapped at the border and we  reached out to countries of origin and  transit in particular iraq to stop the  smuggling  it was good to see  a consensus among us the 27 member  states and the five eastern partners we  unanimously condemn lukaszenko's  behavior and we all stand with the  belarusian people  indeed the second topic was our shared  concern about the russian military  buildup along ukraine's eastern border  we are calling on russia to de-escalate  and refrain from any further aggressive  acts  we assured ukraine of our steadfast  support and commitment to its  sovereignty  and to its territorial integrity  and there should indeed be no doubt the  european union will respond firmly  to any further aggression against  ukraine  the third topic we have been discussing  was the cooperation and in particular  the joint fight against the pandemic  the european union has shared 13 million  doses of vaccine with our eastern  partner and more is to come  the commission has just signed a 35  million a  euro agreement with a polish development  bank  it will allow team europe as a whole to  step up the delivery of vaccines to the  eastern partnership  and we are also ready to provide  vaccines to the people of belarus  we have set aside vaccine doses and we  are right now looking into ways of  delivering them to the belarus people as  quickly as possible  but beyond covet we also support our  partners deal with other pressing  challenges  just before the summit we signed an  emergency support package for moldova  of 60 million euros as you know moldova  faces a severe gas supply crisis  and with this program we help reduce the  impact  of rising gas prices on the most  vulnerable people in moldova  and finally we've been talking about the  deepening cooperation with our partners  across the board  today we've launched a new more  ambitious phase  in our eastern partnership  a new agenda to help us  in our joint work of recovery and reform  underpinned by significant investment  indeed as charles said as a part of our  overall 300 billion euro global gateway  initiative  the economic and investment plan for the  region the eastern partnership could  trigger 17 billion euros of investment  for our eastern partners  there are very interesting impressive  projects in the pipeline for example  making buildings in moldova more energy  efficient  or to expanding high-speed internet to  rural georgia or to rolling out fiber  optic cable under the black sea just to  name a few  so we invited our eastern partners to  get on board  and to work with us on building new  links with us in the shared region thank  you  thank you  madam president we'll first take a few  questions  uh remotely and we'll start with  christian christian to have the ifp  foreign  is  so indeed there are sanctions against  russia in place  and  therefore they are for the actual  situation but indeed  we have been tasked as a commission by  the european council in summer to  prepare different options  in case that further aggression will  occur  so we have done our  homework we have done the preparation  so that we could respond  to further aggression which we hope will  not take place  the format of  negotiating  is very clear  after the annexation of crimea by russia  there has been established the normandy  format  where germany and france are moderators  and observers  and the conflicting parties ukraine and  russia  are dealing with in this format so i  think this is the format for that  conflict  thank you next question is for rickards  from a radio free europe picard you have  the flow  hello uh i'm going to continue with sort  of a similar question to my colleague mr  spielman i wonder actually if  it actually is possible for the european  union  to adopt proactive as opposed to  reactive sanction or does it need to  have to be sort of evidence-based so  russia needs to move before and secondly  um i think it's a third declaration  eastern partnership declaration in a row  now where you acknowledge the european  aspiration of the three associated  countries it's the same uh language all  the time essentially for the last three  summits  do you guys have any ambitions  considering you still have probably one  or two more summits as presidents to  move beyond that to be perhaps a bit  more ambitious when it comes to moldova  georgia and ukraine  yeah for the for the first version of  the imperial trifer we have given a very  clear a very clear answer we can only  repeat what what has been said uh just  before we have sentence in place point  one uh we are ready to take sanctions  additional sanctions if it is needed and  we will assess what will be the  situation and we have expressed and we  will reiterate tomorrow a very firm  position uh we support the intellectual  the total integrity of ukraine we  support the sovereignty of ukraine we  are coordinating with our like-minded  partners with our allies and we will  decide based on what will happen and we  hope that there will be no further  escalation and no further adoration  there is a normal d format in order to  implement  the missed agreement and there are  channels of  communication channels of that that are  needed in order to avoid any further  escalation  so indeed our very first call is on  russia to de-escalate but we are also  prepared for any  increasing aggression from russia's side  as i said sanctions are in place  those sanctions could be tightened but  of course they are also sanctions  prepared  that  are additional and coming on top on all  the different fields you might think of  and the message is very clear  should russia take further aggressive  actions against ukraine  the costs will be severe and the  consequences serious and this is the  clear messaging at  this time  the second part of your question  we have an association agreement and a  deep and controversial comprehensive  free trade agreement  both have a lot of scope still to be  explored in the width and in the depth  so there's a lot of added value we can  still develop  between us  and the trio countries and this is  exactly the job which we are doing right  now  on top comes  the economic and investment  plan which is completely new which is a  completely added value of 17 billion  euros with very interesting pro projects  so this is new and that's what we have  discussed today  i'll take one last question from the  room  alina from belsat please  uh hello  is european union ready to show its  strength and close that loopholes in the  sanctions against the regime of  alexander lukashenka  which about asks svelanikanovska asked  you in her statement in european  parliament  it's about loopholes and sanctions which  regime is using  to avoid the sanctions  so loopholes but should not be in  sanctions and of course we are willing  to look into if those  are there  this is an ongoing process  and  therefore the sanctions are very clear  uh to have an effect  but i also think that in the belarus  situation we saw how effective we can be  when the european union acts united and  rapidly what we exactly did so it was  visible that the outrage to the  countries of origin origin did really  work  that the immediate sanctions the united  posture towards belarus did have quite  an effect so that many thousand migrants  now are back safe in their the countries  of origin  and  the sanctions in place are effective  thank you madam president and mr  president  please read our joint declaration which  has been published on our website thank  you and see you tomorrow  foreign  you

